WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.598
Capability compares two distributions. One belongs to the

2
00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:08.390
customer, one belongs to the process, and they

3
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.852
are drawn on the same axis in millimetres.

4
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.374
The customer speaks in limits. Anything between these two

5
00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:20.106
lines is accepted, and nothing outside them is.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.870
The process answers with a spread. It never read the

7
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.988
drawing, and at this width it does not fit.

8
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:31.152
Now improve the process and watch the only number

9
00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:35.746
that matters here: the tolerance divided by six sigma.

10
00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:39.319
That ratio is Cp, and it is pure geometry.

11
00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:44.267
One point three three. The natural spread is now three quarters

12
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:48.196
of the tolerance, and there is room on both sides.

13
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:51.465
Now let the mean drift, and change nothing

14
00:00:51.565 --> 00:00:54.903
else. The spread stays exactly where it was.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.425
Cpk keeps the smaller of the two one sided ratios,

16
00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:03.859
because the near limit is the one you fail first.

17
00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:07.932
0.80, and the near side is now leaking parts.

18
00:01:08.016 --> 00:01:13.287
That leak is 8,198 parts per million, and at this scale you

19
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:19.114
cannot see it. So stretch the vertical axis until it is visible.

20
00:01:19.214 --> 00:01:24.212
The peak leaves the frame; the tail is what we came for.

21
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:32.456
8,198 parts per million is the same sentence as 1 in 122 parts.

22
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:37.202
And the index itself is only the near gap measured in three sigmas.

23
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.232
Which for this drifted process is 0.80.

24
00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:47.161
Every Cpk value is a promise about defect rate, and

25
00:01:47.261 --> 00:01:51.796
the promise is steep. Parts per million up the side,

26
00:01:51.896 --> 00:01:56.608
on a logarithmic scale, because it spans five decades.

27
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.936
Walk Cpk upward from zero point six. Every figure here

28
00:02:01.036 --> 00:02:05.032
is computed at render time by the same function the

29
00:02:05.132 --> 00:02:08.888
test suite checks — nothing is read off a table.

30
00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:16.618
Cpk 1.00, the number that means the limit sits three sigma

31
00:02:16.718 --> 00:02:23.019
out: 1,350 parts per million, or 1 in 741 parts.

32
00:02:23.116 --> 00:02:30.243
Cpk 1.33, the number most customers ask for: 33.04

33
00:02:30.343 --> 00:02:36.024
parts per million, or 1 in 30,269 parts.

34
00:02:36.116 --> 00:02:41.528
Cpk 1.67, and the curve is falling off a cliff:

35
00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:47.392
0.27 parts per million, or 1 in 3.7 million parts.

36
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:51.673
Which is why the difference between one point three three

37
00:02:51.773 --> 00:02:55.511
and one point six seven is not a rounding argument.

38
00:02:55.611 --> 00:02:58.447
It is two orders of magnitude of scrap.

39
00:02:58.533 --> 00:03:02.630
A capability index is a defect rate wearing a friendlier

40
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.977
number. Change the formula and this curve changes with it.

